Measurement is far better now (ties to slides 4, 5, 6)
1993: Safelite had just discovered it could count installs per tech. Effort was invisible and output was the only signal.
Today (general knowledge): GPS, app timestamps, job-completion photos and customer ratings make time, travel and quality observable.
Discussion: If you could observe effort almost perfectly, would you still need piece rate? Better measurement weakens the moral hazard problem but adds new ones: gaming the metric, surveillance resentment, and paying for what’s easy to track instead of what matters.


Piece rate is now normal work (ties to slides 8, 14)
1993: Gassiot said it was “hard” to change an industry built on hourly wages.
Today (general knowledge): Gig platforms like Uber and DoorDash made per-task pay familiar, and algorithms now do the dispatching.
Discussion: The techs’ complaint that DCC managers assigned jobs “equitably” instead of to the fastest workers is exactly what an algorithm would now do. It would also raise the fairness questions on your compensation fairness slide.


Legal guardrails (ties to slides 12, 14)
General knowledge, verify before stating: Piece-rate pay still has to meet minimum wage and overtime rules, and some states (California is the usual example) require separate pay for non-productive time and rest breaks.
Discussion: A guarantee that was a design choice in 1993 may be partly a legal floor today, so the 30% cut would face more constraints.

Federal: The Department of Labor says piece-rate earnings are allowed, but the regular rate cannot be less than the minimum wage. Overtime for piece-rate workers is computed from an average hourly rate (total weekly pay divided by hours worked).
California: AB 1513 requires piece-rate employers to pay separately for rest and recovery periods and other nonproductive time, shown separately on the wage statement. The exception is when the employer pays at least minimum wage for every hour worked. The state’s fact sheet doesn’t give an effective date, so say “since about 2016” or skip the year.
How to say it in the room: “Piece-rate pay still has to clear minimum wage and overtime rules, and some states add more.” That is safe, and I would not say it limits what Safelite could do in 1993. The federal rule applied then too. The new part is state-level add-ons.


Information and outside options (ties to slide 13)
1993: “Buck and a truck” poaching worked through word of mouth and recruiters.
Today (general knowledge): Posted wages and job-board listings make the outside option visible instantly, so workers are likely more sensitive to small pay gaps.
Discussion: That strengthens the retention risk of cutting the floor. It also changes the “30% pay cut” rumor problem, since a bad framing now travels fast.


The job is more complex (ties to slide 16, multitasking and quality)
General knowledge: Modern windshields often carry cameras and sensors that need recalibration, so jobs vary more and safety stakes are higher.
Discussion: Units per day was already a crude metric in 1993. Today a unit can mean very different amounts of work, which makes paying per unit more prone to quality shortcuts.


Money has changed
Rough calculation: Prices are about double 1993 levels, so the $11/hr example is roughly $23-24/hr in today’s dollars (approximate, from CPI, not in the case). A $132-a-week cut then would hurt, but the household risk and the idea that you can’t absorb it are the same.


What hasn’t changed
Output is still a noisy signal of effort, wrong parts and bad scheduling still sit outside the tech’s control, and insurance vs. incentives is still the core tradeoff. If someone says “this is dated,” that’s the answer: the technology changed, not the economics.
Optional: what actually happened

The case stops before results. Lazear’s later study of Safelite found a 44% increase in output per worker, with only about half from the incentive effect on existing workers and the rest from workforce composition (better hires). Turnover rose slightly but wasn’t selective, so most selection came through hiring. Profits also rose. That’s a good reveal after your final vote, since it supports your selection and effort slides, but it spoils the decision if shown before. I’d keep it for Q&A. The source didn’t give a pay or quality number, so don’t cite one.

